### CommitFailedError

Source: https://github.com/delta-io/delta-rs/blob/main/docs/api/exceptions.md

Raised when a commit operation to the Delta Lake transaction log fails, often due to concurrent modifications.

```APIDOC
## CommitFailedError

### Description
Exception raised when a commit to the Delta Lake transaction log fails. This can occur due to optimistic concurrency control failures, where the table has been modified by another operation since the read.

### Inheritance
Inherits from `DeltaError`.
```

### How Delta Lake implements transactions

Source: https://github.com/delta-io/delta-rs/blob/main/docs/how-delta-lake-works/delta-lake-acid-transactions.md

Delta Lake employs a non-locking MVCC (multi-version concurrency control) strategy where writers optimistically create new data and check for conflicts only at the end of the transaction. If a conflict is detected, the transaction is abandoned, preventing data corruption. This contrasts with a locking approach that would acquire a lock at the beginning.

--------------------------------

### ACID Transactions > Atomic transactions

Source: https://github.com/delta-io/delta-rs/blob/main/docs/how-delta-lake-works/delta-lake-acid-transactions.md

Delta Lake transactions are atomic, meaning they either fully complete or fully fail. If a job writing to a Delta table fails partway through, no data is added, and the partial write is ignored. This is unlike traditional data lakes where partial writes can occur and are difficult to manage.

--------------------------------

### How Delta Lake transactions can conflict

Source: https://github.com/delta-io/delta-rs/blob/main/docs/how-delta-lake-works/delta-lake-acid-transactions.md

Transaction conflicts occur when multiple operations attempt to modify the same data files concurrently. Delta Lake detects these conflicts by checking the transaction log. If a conflict is found, the later transaction is aborted and its newly written data files are ignored, preventing data inconsistency. These orphaned files can be cleaned up later using vacuum operations.

### Write Validation with Generated Columns

Source: https://github.com/delta-io/delta-rs/blob/main/crates/core/src/operations/write/execution.rs

During write execution, generated columns are validated through DataValidationExec. The validation predicates are constructed from the table's generated column definitions, ensuring computed values meet constraints before writing.

```rust
let mut validations = if let Some(snapshot) = snapshot {
    validation_predicates(
        session,
        &plan.schema().to_dfschema()?,
        snapshot.table_configuration(),
    )?
} else {
    debug!(
        "Using plan schema to derive generated columns, since no snapshot was provided. Implies first write."
    );
    let delta_schema: StructType = schema.as_ref().try_into_kernel()?;
    let df_schema = schema.clone().to_dfschema()?;
    generated_columns_to_exprs(session, &df_schema, &delta_schema.get_generated_columns()?)?
};
```

